= Solution
{parent=a}

Label the vertices $S$ (left), $A$ (upper middle), $B$ (lower middle), $C$ (upper right), $T$ (lower right). Use the <flow balance> convention outgoing minus incoming equals supply. The printed initial flows, in the order $(SA,SB,AB,AC,BC,BT,CT)$, are
$$
x^{(0)}=(25,0,15,10,10,5,20).
$$
They obey all <capacity constraints> and the <flow balances> $(25,0,0,0,-25)$. The four strictly interior edges $SA,AB,BC,BT$ form a <spanning tree>. The remaining edges are at a bound: $SB$ at zero, $AC$ and $CT$ at their upper bounds. Thus this is already a feasible <network simplex tree basis>; no artificial feasibility phase is needed. Its total cost is $340$.

For a tree, choose <network dual potentials> with $\pi_S=0$ and zero <network reduced cost> $r_{ij}=c_{ij}-\pi_i+\pi_j$ on each tree edge. Initially
$$
(\pi_S,\pi_A,\pi_B,\pi_C,\pi_T)=(0,-7,-12,-14,-14).
$$
The nonbasic lower-bound edge $SB$ has <network reduced cost> $-6$. Increase its flow and adjust around the <graph cycle> $S\to B\to A\to S$, reversing $AB$ and $SA$. The available step is $\min(20,15,25)=15$, so $AB$ leaves at zero. This gives
$$
x^{(1)}=(10,15,0,10,10,5,20),\qquad \text{cost}=250.
$$
The tree is now $SA,SB,BC,BT$, and the potentials are $(0,-7,-6,-8,-8)$.

Now $AC$ is at its upper bound but has <network reduced cost> $3>0$, so decreasing it improves cost. Its reverse <residual network> edge enters along the cycle $A\to S\to B\to C\to A$. The forward edges $SB,BC$ increase; $SA,AC$ decrease. The step is
$$
\min(x_{SA},m_{SB}-x_{SB},m_{BC}-x_{BC},x_{AC})
=\min(10,5,10,10)=5.
$$
Thus $SB$ leaves at its upper bound, and
$$
x^{(2)}=(5,20,0,5,15,5,20),\qquad \text{cost}=235.
$$
The new tree $SA,AC,BC,BT$ has potentials $(0,-7,-9,-11,-11)$.

The upper-bound edge $CT$ now has <network reduced cost> $1>0$. Enter its reverse direction along $T\to C\to B\to T$, decreasing $CT$ and $BC$ and increasing $BT$. The step is $\min(20,15,25-5)=15$, so $BC$ leaves at zero. The resulting flow is
$$
\boxed{x^*=(5,20,0,5,0,20,5),\qquad \text{minimum cost}=220.}
$$
The tree is $SA,AC,BT,CT$, with potentials $(0,-7,-10,-11,-12)$. The nonbasic edges have <network reduced costs> $r_{SB}=-4$ at its upper bound, $r_{AB}=2$ at its lower bound, and $r_{BC}=1$ at its lower bound. All have the correct signs, so there is no improving <network simplex> pivot. The next part proves these signs certify optimality.

